[b]Speculative Markets 1.2.0[/b]

[list]
[*]Interest groups support roughly two traded goods and may oppose one. Their approval changes with booms, busts and interventions in those goods. Powerful groups react twice as strongly. Marginalized groups have no effect. Stances show on the interest group panel and Speculation tab.
[*]The new Monetary Standard law group offers Bimetallism, the Gold Standard and Fiat Money. Bimetallism makes rescues cheaper but boom endings harder. Gold cuts loan interest and trust losses but lengthens panics. Fiat makes rescues cheaper and panics shorter while feeding speculation. Laissez-Faire strengthens gold and Interventionism strengthens fiat.
[*]Financial Integration, a new power bloc principle group with three levels. It needs Sphere of Influence. A panic in one member costs the others 25% less Market Confidence. From the second level, members' Lending Limit rises by half the months by which the leader's exceeds theirs. At the third, the leader's Exchange counts its members' capital twice, up to its own.
[*]Investors may favor or shun a good after six months of prices 10% or more above or below its base price. A term lasts a year and fades over its last six months. A Favored good gains Investor Interest and a Shunned one loses it. A gold or red disc under a good's icon marks a term.
[*]Each good belongs to one of nine market sectors. If its sector was flat or moving the other way last month, a price swing can have as little as half its usual effect on Investor Interest. The goods table groups goods under collapsible sector headings.
[*]The Speculation tab has a new Effects on Our Economy section. It shows what the market adds to or takes from weekly investment, yearly GDP, construction and trade.
[*]Market Confidence shows what to expect next month. It also appears beside GDP on the top bar.
[*]1.2 overrides the top bar, alert row and interest group panel. Other top bar mods such as The Great Rework need a merge.
[*]The Speculation goods list has a Risk column, tier icons and a Trend column. Filters pick a sector, an interest level, Favored or Shunned goods, or goods an interest group supports or opposes.
[*]Domestic Market News groups feverish speculation, boom endings and interest group stance changes by market sector in one bulletin.
[*]Settling a contest frees its slot, and each win or loss has its own notice naming the opposing Exchange.
[*]Paths to Renown rank Exchanges pursuing the speciality. Rankings refresh monthly, ties favor the holder and a new leader takes over immediately. AI Exchanges consider a stronger speciality when a slot unlocks.
[/list]

[b]Balance[/b]
[list]
[*]Market Confidence starts at 50 and returns toward 50 when left alone (was 55). Banks now lend into the investment pool above 50 (was 55).
[*]Each booming good adds up to 6 Market Confidence a month (was 0.85), with all goods together capped at +3 per month before multipliers. Confidence closes 20% of the gap to its resting level each month (was 4%). A month of boom endings takes at most 35 (was 40).
[*]A collapse after a Feverish boom costs a sixth more Market Confidence (7 per point of hype, was 6).
[*]Speculative Excess starts building at 80 confidence (was 75) and grows by how far confidence is above 80 (was 70), so 5 a month at 85 (was 15). A burst is no longer capped at 40 confidence (45 on High volatility), and what confidence cannot cover becomes Bad Debts.
[*]Panics last about 3 months before other effects (was 6.5).
[*]Each month of leverage past 3 adds 10% to a panic's cut to private construction, so all of it goes at 8 months (was always half), and 10% to its length, up to 90% (was none). A bank rescue halves the cut and caps the remaining term at half its length.
[*]A panic calls back about 53% of outstanding credit at Strict lending standards, 75% at Sound and 98% at Reckless (was always 50%), and the debt falls by the same share.
[*]Each whole month of leverage adds 3.5% building throughput, up to 14% at 4 months (was 2%, up to 24% at 12 months).
[*]Ruinous leverage raises the Panic Threshold by up to 50 (was 40). An economy with no financial depth has its Panic Threshold 14 lower (was 5). Buy Up Bad Loans clears 30% of Bad Debts (was half).
[*]Each point of Bad Debts costs 3.5% throughput (was 0.4%), with at most 20 points. The worst cut 70% throughput and 15% investment at their peak (was about 13% and 25%). Once fully felt, they clear by 0.47 points per month under Normal or Loose Credit and 1.5 under Tight Credit.
[*]Banks lend less the more Bad Debts they carry, and nothing at or above 11 at the Sound midpoint (was nothing while any Bad Debts lasted).
[*]Banks start in the middle of Sound lending standards (was the cautious edge of Sound) and turn stricter after a boom's losses a third as fast. Lending Standards swing lending by 35% (was 25%).
[*]Financial District workers' standard of living follows Market Confidence. It is 3 lower while investors are Shaken and 3 higher in a Mania (was 1 higher in a Mania only).
[*]Booms are full size from the start (were 70% before the finance technologies). Stock Exchange makes them 24.5% larger and Investment Banks 28% (was 36% and 43%).
[*]An intervention ties up at most 50 Authority per good. A favored good's boom or bust moves approval for 180 days. Distrusted Exchanges still pay higher loan interest.
[*]Loan interest cuts could add up past 100% and make loans free. Several stacking sources of cheaper loans are removed. Converted Debt cuts interest by 10% (was 20%) and ends once debt falls below 20% of the credit limit.
[*]Sound Lending gives 2% to 8% Shopkeepers' investment pool efficiency by place (was 1.5% to 6% cheaper loans). First place in Lender to Nations gives 10% leverage resistance (was 5% cheaper loans).
[*]Up to 8 blue chips count, each in full (was the first 5 in full and the next 5 at half). Each adds 0.4% to the Capitalists' investment pool contribution (was 0.6%).
[*]Switching to another Exchange charter costs 25% of trust (was 50%).
[*]Investor interest gives a good's makers 2%, 4% and 6% throughput at Keen, Eager and Feverish (was 3%, 5% and 8%).
[*]Encourage and Restrain now scale with Market Confidence instead of aiming at Keen and Calm. Restrain never goes below Calm. A good's intervention can change once every 3 months.
[*]A Great Issue adds up to 25% to the Exchange score, in full only for a good with 3% or more of trade (was 25% for any good).
[*]An Exchange can run 1 contest at Provincial Standing, 2 at National, 4 at International and 6 at World Centre (was 1, 2, 3 and 4).
[/list]

Britain starts on the Gold Standard and every other country on Bimetallism. On an existing save, interest group stances and monetary laws arrive at the next monthly update. Existing Bad Debts convert once to preserve their throughput penalty, up to the new maximum of 70%.

